      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50257409",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Long context windows often promise infinite memory for autonomous agents, but cumulative multi-turn interactions frequently trigger sudden reasoning collapse. When Key-Value caches saturate and attention dilutes, agents drop critical constraints and hallucinate without warning. Most teams attempt to solve this with prompt-based evaluators, but self-reflection prompts introduce severe latency and suffer from the exact blind spots they try to catch.\n\nA more resilient approach focuses on external telemetry rather than semantic inspection. Real-time tracking of generation metrics reveals that cognitive degradation is preceded by unmistakable mathematical shifts. Specifically, the local coefficient of variation drops sharply from 16.6 percent down to 3.8 percent, while the correlation between latency and output tokens surges to positive 0.985.\n\nEvaluating these phase transitions at the model boundary allows harnesses to detect failures with sub-millisecond overhead. When metrics deteriorate, systems can trigger an autonomous deterministic rollback to the last stable state before errors propagate.\n\nProtocol telemetry turns unpredictable agent degradation into an actionable infrastructure circuit breaker."
    },
    {
      "title": "PostgreSQL replication slot and Debezium offset form one distributed checkpoint",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://shiftmag.dev/how-to-survive-database-failover-debezium-and-postgresql-in-production-11676/",
      "date": "2026-10-27",
      "authors": [
        "Frane Jelavic"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how PostgreSQL 16, Patroni, and Debezium interact during failover to prevent silent data loss or event replay gaps in transactional outbox architectures.",
      "hn_id": "50259372",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50259372",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Getting Change Data Capture working with Debezium is easy during normal operations, but database failovers reveal subtle distributed state issues.\n\nIn a PostgreSQL cluster managed by Patroni, promoting a standby breaks the assumption that Debezium can simply reconnect. If you recreate a logical replication slot after promotion, the new slot may start beyond Debezium's last durable offset in Kafka Connect. This creates an unrecoverable gap in your event stream.\n\nThe core insight is that PostgreSQL's logical replication slot and Debezium's Kafka Connect offset form a single distributed checkpoint. They cannot be managed independently. When designing transactional outbox pipelines, your failover automation must synchronize slot state across replicas to preserve exact LSN positioning.\n\nFailing over safely requires coordinating offset commitments before promoting standbys, rather than attempting ad-hoc recovery after the cutover occurs. Reliability lives in the seam between the storage engine and the streaming consumer. Checkpoint synchronization is essential for true at-least-once delivery."
    },
