      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50262852",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most personal websites run on static hosts, but treating one as a production-grade infrastructure lab provides great lessons in zero-trust architecture. kalinowski.io runs on a fully private Azure Kubernetes Service cluster that exposes zero inbound public ports. Every external packet reaches the cluster exclusively through an outbound tunnel established by a Cloudflare connector pod.\n\nThe cluster topology handles traffic by routing through an internal Nginx pod alongside five targeted Go microservices. Instead of granting wide cloud permissions, each microservice receives isolated Azure Entra ID credentials with tightly scoped boundaries. For example, the newsletter service holds write-only permissions to Azure Table Storage, preventing reading or extracting existing subscriber records even if the pod gets compromised.\n\nAll cluster state is declared in Git and deployed continuously via Flux, while runtime secrets flow through Azure Key Vault via the CSI driver. This design demonstrates how to implement zero-trust ingress and microsegmentation cleanly on a modest infrastructure budget.\n\nBuilding out over-engineered systems under tight constraints is often the best way to master modern cloud-native security patterns."
    },
    {
