      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50265240",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most multi-agent conversational bots break down in production because webhook timeouts and state drift make conversational turns unreliable. Decoupling ingress, state memory, and cognition into distinct layers transforms fragile agent loops into resilient systems.\n\nIn this prototype architecture running on Google Cloud, an omni-channel webhook pushes raw payloads directly into Cloud Pub/Sub, separating message delivery from LLM inference. LangGraph handles the agentic state machine, orchestrating Gemini models alongside deterministic financial tools. By decoupling these layers, the platform avoids dropping messages even when multi-turn model latency spikes under heavy traffic.\n\nA recurring issue with agent swarms is non-deterministic behavior during transactional steps such as checkout or inventory allocation. Moving financial calculations outside the agent prompt into isolated, deterministic tool nodes ensures that mathematical precision and ledger updates never rely on model guesswork.\n\nSeparating transport ingress from cognitive state execution is the cleanest pattern for building production-grade agent swarms."
