      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50260033",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Asking a single AI agent to review a pull request end to end often results in failure. When an LLM must evaluate syntax, runtime edge cases, architectural boundaries, and product intent all within one prompt window, it loses the ability to zoom in and out effectively.\n\nRecent high-throughput engineering teams have had to adapt as code volume explodes. At monorepo scales handling tens of thousands of automated daily changes, the solution is not a larger context window. Instead, it is decomposing verification into distinct abstraction layers. Staging checks across separated concerns cut revert rates by more than 40 percent.\n\nHuman engineers do not inspect code uniformly. We read a function line by line for local safety, step back to examine the interface contract, and step back further to ensure domain consistency. Automated review pipelines must mirror this cognitive structure.\n\nSplitting automated reviews into explicit abstraction stages prevents token distraction and produces substantially higher signal."
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimizing Data Transfer Between DuckDB and Dotnet Applications",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.giorgi.dev/database/duckdb-net-1-5-6-1-data-reader-performance/",
      "date": "2026-10-27",
      "authors": [
        "Giorgi Dalakishvili"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how low-level optimizations in data marshaling between C and .NET can yield massive throughput increases. It provides concrete architectural insights into cutting memory allocations and accelerating query readers.",
