      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50259575",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Database performance bottlenecks do not always stem from query engines or disk I/O. When bridging unmanaged analytical engines to managed runtimes, the data marshaling layer often becomes the primary bottleneck.\n\nDuckDB.NET demonstrated this clearly in their 1.5.6.1 release. Running identical queries on the exact same underlying DuckDB engine produced typed read throughput improvements between 3.5x and 6x. Appending date and time values achieved a 26x speedup, slashing memory allocations from 2.8 GB down to less than 0.1 MB for a million records.\n\nThese massive improvements were achieved entirely within the managed ADO.NET provider rather than the C library. Eliminating defensive boxing, minimizing intermediate copies, and aligning memory buffers transformed client throughput without touching engine internals.\n\nOptimizing client-side marshaling yields massive performance gains before query optimization even enters the picture."
    },
    {
