    {
      "title": "Compile-time code generation enables zero-allocation Java resilience",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/fabiangftech/resilience4cata",
      "date": "2026-10-27",
      "authors": [
        "fabiangftech"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand how compile-time code generation eliminates proxy overhead and reflection for standard resilience primitives. You will learn how straight-line code can enforce fault tolerance with zero memory allocations on success paths.",
      "hn_id": "50261924",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50261924",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Traditional resilience frameworks in Java rely heavily on reflection, dynamic proxies, and runtime interceptors. While libraries such as Resilience4j simplify retries, bulkheads, and circuit breakers, their runtime indirection introduces heap allocations and latency jitter that punish high-throughput services.\n\nResilience4cata addresses this bottleneck by shifting resilience instrumentation from runtime reflection to compile-time code generation. Using an annotation processor targeting Java 21, it generates straightforward, direct method invocations wrapped in allocation-free policy protocol calls. Because the code is emitted at compile time, the execution path incurs low-nanosecond overhead and achieves zero heap allocations whenever calls succeed.\n\nBeyond standard retries and circuit breakers, the framework packages adaptive concurrency limits, hedging, and rate limiting into plain Java without requiring Spring, AspectJ, or bytecode agents. It supports both synchronous workflows and asynchronous CompletionStage pipelines while retaining configuration overrides at runtime.\n\nEliminating runtime reflection is one of the most reliable ways to guarantee predictable p99 latencies in modern service architectures."
    },
    {
      "title": "S3 costs for Iceberg CDC stem from requests not bytes",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://etleap.com/blog/understanding-iceberg-cdc-costs-on-s3-requests-not-bytes",
      "date": "2026-10-27",
      "authors": [
        "Benedict Chan",
        "Archie Menzies"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn why streaming CDC into Apache Iceberg drives S3 costs through API request volume rather than byte volume, and how commit frequency escalates expenses.",
      "hn_id": "50259019",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50259019",
      "image": null,
      "content": "When streaming change data capture into Apache Iceberg on Amazon S3, your cloud storage bill is almost never driven by gigabytes written. Instead, it is dominated by API request volume.\n\nEvery single Iceberg commit writes new metadata files, manifests, and snapshot pointers. If you push for near real-time freshness by committing micro-batches every few seconds, the number of PUT and GET requests explodes. S3 Tier 2 requests can quietly account for over eighty percent of your total infrastructure expenditure while data volume remains completely flat.\n\nThe fix requires rethinking the tension between data freshness and storage semantics. Grouping writes into larger batches, aligning commit intervals to minute-level windows rather than sub-second intervals, and auditing access logs by requester can slash your monthly storage spend without degrading downstream query performance.\n\nObject stores are built for high throughput at scale, but their pricing model heavily penalizes transactional metadata churn."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cost-coupled Gittins efficiently finds Pareto optimal agent assignments",
