      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50255870",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Assigning different LLMs to distinct roles in an agentic workflow usually devolves into expensive guesswork. If you have four or five specialized roles and want to balance execution cost, latency, and task accuracy, exhaustively testing every combination across hundreds of candidate assignments rapidly explodes your evaluation budget.\n\nResearchers have developed Cost-Coupled Gittins (CC-Gittins), a multi-objective search algorithm tailored for multi-agent workflows. The core insight leverages evaluation-cost coupling: configurations that are expensive to deploy in production are inherently expensive to benchmark during testing. By alternating searches between task quality and deployment cost while sharing evaluation data across passes, the algorithm models uncertainty to locate optimal role allocations.\n\nEmpirical benchmarks show that CC-Gittins reconstructs a substantial portion of the true Pareto frontier using only 10 percent of the exhaustive evaluation cost, reaching near-complete frontier coverage at 30 percent. For engineering teams orchestrating multi-agent systems, this provides a mathematically rigorous way to slash model serving bills without degrading output quality.\n\nTreating agent model assignment as an active learning problem is far more sustainable than intuition-driven model selection."
    },
    {
