      "content": "Generic LLM serving engines prioritize cross-architecture compatibility, but that portability often leaves performance on the table. A specialized server called imp demonstrates the throughput gains possible when you drop backwards compatibility and optimize strictly for NVIDIA Blackwell hardware.\n\nBy focusing exclusively on the sm_120a instruction set, the engine manages to deliver 96.3 tokens per second on a single RTX 5090 running Qwen3.8-27B. That represents a 1.38x throughput improvement over standard vLLM without speculative decoding, and a 1.23x speedup compared to llama.cpp running on the identical GGUF weight format.\n\nThe project also demonstrates aggressive offloading strategies, successfully serving a 123.6 GiB model variant from a 32 GB card while maintaining over 90 tokens per second. It packages both OpenAI and Anthropic compatible APIs into a single containerized binary, showing how deep kernel specialization can outpace generic multi-backend inference engines.\n\nTargeting single microarchitectures instead of lowest-common-denominator runtimes unlocks significant headroom."
    },
    {
      "title": "AI eliminates software abstractions designed for human cognitive limits",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://vithushansylvester.com/writing/what-is-left-when-the-programmer-is-not-human",
