      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50258168",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most modern software abstractions exist primarily to accommodate human cognitive limitations. We rely on high-level languages, heavy runtimes, and opinionated frameworks because human developers cannot hold assembly state in memory or manage manual memory safely at scale.\n\nWhen an autonomous agent writes software, those ergonomics become unnecessary overhead. In an experiment called HarvestChain, an agent authored a complete graphical game compiled to a 40 kilobyte WebAssembly binary. The output consists of over ten thousand lines of raw WebAssembly text across 681 functions, with zero external dependencies, zero image files, and all graphics rendered through integer arithmetic.\n\nSafety did not come from static typing or manual code review. Instead, the author shifted all human effort into the verification harness: 705 automated tests and strict continuous integration budgets that banned floating-point operations.\n\nThe future of agentic engineering is not about reading generated code, but about constructing unbreakable harnesses that define correct behavior."
